Subject: Keep the `const` an elaborated type carries

A `const` referent written with a qualified name loses the qualifier:
`void f(const std::int32_t&)` comes out `*mut c_int`, a mutable pointer to
something C++ will not let anyone write to, while the `const int&` beside it
comes out `*const c_int` as it should. Anything which takes the address of
such a function - a member pointer, a function pointer - then names a
signature the function does not have, so the loss is not cosmetic.

clang wraps a qualified name in `CXType_Elaborated`, and `Type::from_clang_ty`
deals with one by recursing on `clang_Type_getNamedType`. That call answers
with the type the name denotes and none of the cv-qualifiers the elaborated
node carries, so the recursion sees a bare `int32_t`. Every reader of the
qualifier sits downstream of that point - the tail of `from_clang_ty` takes
`is_const` from whichever type the recursion ended on, and `TryToRustTy`
renders a pointer or a reference `*const` or `*mut` from the pointee item's
flag - so the `const` is gone for good. The same alias named without its
namespace is not elaborated at all, which is why only one of the two spellings
was wrong.

Put the qualifier back on what the recursion produced. A newly parsed type is
this use's own node and can carry it directly. A type which resolved to an
existing item cannot: that item is shared with every other use of the name,
most of them not `const`, so the qualifier goes on a `ResolvedTypeRef` wrapper
instead - which is what `build_const_wrapper` is for, and what clang's own
qualified nodes look like.

`volatile` rides on the same node. The wrapper path already carries it:
`build_wrapper` copies the qualifier off the type it is handed, so a
`const volatile` referent which resolved to an existing item keeps both. The
newly-parsed path restores only `const`, and a `volatile` referent without
`const` returns before any of this runs - restoring that qualifier in its own
right is a decision for whoever needs it, not for a patch about `const`.

--- a/ir/ty.rs
+++ b/ir/ty.rs
@@ -1451,13 +1451,45 @@
                     );
                 }
                 CXType_Elaborated => {
-                    return Self::from_clang_ty(
+                    // `clang_Type_getNamedType` answers with the type the name
+                    // denotes and none of the cv-qualifiers the elaborated node
+                    // carries: `const std::int32_t` names a plain `int32_t`. The
+                    // tail of this function reads the qualifier off whichever
+                    // type the recursion ended on, so recursing alone drops it,
+                    // and a `const` pointee then reaches codegen as `*mut T`.
+                    let named = ty.named();
+                    let result = Self::from_clang_ty(
                         potential_id,
-                        &ty.named(),
+                        &named,
                         location,
                         parent_id,
                         ctx,
-                    );
+                    )?;
+                    if !ty.is_const() || named.is_const() {
+                        return Ok(result);
+                    }
+                    return Ok(match result {
+                        ParseResult::New(mut inner, decl) => {
+                            inner.is_const = true;
+                            ParseResult::New(inner, decl)
+                        }
+                        // The name resolved to an item every other use of it
+                        // shares, so the qualifier cannot be written onto that
+                        // item; it goes on a wrapper, which is what wrapping
+                        // exists for here.
+                        ParseResult::AlreadyResolved(id) => {
+                            let with_id = ctx.next_item_id();
+                            ParseResult::AlreadyResolved(
+                                ctx.build_const_wrapper(
+                                    with_id,
+                                    id.as_type_id_unchecked(),
+                                    parent_id,
+                                    ty,
+                                )
+                                .into(),
+                            )
+                        }
+                    });
                 }
                 CXType_ObjCId => TypeKind::ObjCId,
                 CXType_ObjCSel => TypeKind::ObjCSel,
